const manonaArticleTwoBody: ContentBlock[] = `I. The Teacher


In 1960, Wele Cecil Manona entered the classroom — not as a student, but as a teacher. He was twenty-three years old. He had come through Healdtown in its great days, absorbed its discipline, its English, its conviction that education was a form of service. Now, like every black graduate of mission education in the early 1960s, he faced the institution that would define his professional life for the next decade: Bantu Education.

The Bantu Education Act of 1953 had begun the gradual transfer of black schooling from the mission societies to the Department of Bantu Education. By 1960, the transition was well advanced. The mission schools that had produced Mandela, Sobukwe, Gqubule, and Manona himself were being swallowed by a system designed not to educate but to subordinate. The curriculum was narrowed. The standards were lowered. The purpose, as Hendrik Verwoerd had articulated it with chilling clarity, was to prepare black children for the subordinate roles the state had assigned them in the economy. "There is no place for him [the Bantu] in the European community above the level of certain forms of labour," Verwoerd had told Parliament in 1953 (Republic of South Africa, 1953).

Manona became a teacher inside this system. The details of where he taught are scarce — the record tells us he began his career as a school teacher in 1960 (Wits University Press, 2024) — but the fact itself is significant. He was a minister's son, a Healdtown graduate, a young man who had been raised in the conviction that education was a covenantal act, now working inside a system designed to strip education of its covenantal force.

He taught. He did not rebel. He did not flee. He did what his father's ministry and his mother's discipline had taught him to do: he worked, honestly and quietly, inside the institution he had been given. But the institution was narrowing. The space for independent black intellectual formation was closing. And Manona, who had been trained at Healdtown to believe that education was a form of service, was now inside a system that had redefined education as a form of control.

The tension between these two definitions — education as service and education as control — would shape the next decade of his life. It would drive him, eventually, out of the classroom and into the studio.

II. The Escape

Professor Michael Whisson, in his tribute to Manona after his death in 2013, used a striking phrase. He wrote that Manona "escaped this fate by joining the SABC Xhosa radio service" (Whisson, 2013). The phrase is Whisson's, not Manona's, but it carries the weight of a life-altering decision. Bantu Education was a "fate." The SABC was an "escape." The language tells us something about how the choice was understood, from the inside, by those who lived it.

The South African Broadcasting Corporation was, in the 1960s, the only national broadcaster in the country. It was a state institution, controlled by the National Party government, and its programming reflected the political architecture of apartheid. But within that architecture, there were spaces — narrow, contested, but real — where black voices could be heard. The Xhosa radio service was one of those spaces.

The SABC had been broadcasting in African languages since the 1940s. Broadcast House, the corporation's headquarters on Commissioner Street in Johannesburg, had been built in 1935–36 for I.W. Schlesinger's African Broadcasting Company and taken over by the SABC when it was established in 1936 (Artefacts.co.za). By the 1960s, the SABC operated multiple language services, including isiXhosa, and these services employed black announcers, producers, and scriptwriters. The work was constrained — the state controlled the content, the tone, and the boundaries — but it was work that required voice, language, and presence. It was, for a man like Manona, a different kind of classroom.

He joined the SABC Xhosa radio service. The decision was strategic. It moved him out of the Bantu Education classroom and into a profession where his voice — the voice that had been trained in the mission school, refined at Healdtown, shaped by the cadences of rural isiXhosa and the discipline of Methodist English — could become his instrument. The teacher became the broadcaster. The classroom gave way to the studio.",

III. The Radio Voice

At the SABC, Manona became very well known as a disc jockey. Whisson's tribute records this fact with characteristic warmth: "He became very well known as a disc jockey — which was to stand him in good stead later when his greeting in many strange places brought the immediate and admiring response, 'Manona!'" (Whisson, 2013).

The detail is not trivial. To be a disc jockey on the SABC Xhosa radio service in the 1960s was to be a public voice — a voice that entered homes, shebeens, taxis, and shebeen-rooms across the Eastern Cape and beyond. In a society where black public presence was systematically restricted — where black voices were excluded from the press, the parliament, and the academy — the radio was one of the few institutions where a black voice could reach a black audience at scale. The disc jockey was not merely an entertainer. He was a presence. He was a familiar sound in unfamiliar times.

Manona's obituary in the Rhodes University archive described him as a "radio celebrity" (Rhodes University, 2013). The word is carefully chosen. Celebrity, in the context of 1960s black South Africa, did not mean fame in the American sense. It meant recognition. It meant that people who had never met him knew his name, knew his voice, and felt, when they heard him on the radio, a kind of companionship. The Eastern Cape was — and remains — a region where radio is woven into the texture of daily life. To be a voice on that radio was to be present in the lives of people you would never meet. It was a form of public service, conducted through the medium of sound.

The work also sharpened the skills that would define his later career. The broadcaster reads the air. The broadcaster must be attentive to tone, to pace, to the weight of a pause. The broadcaster must listen even as he speaks, because the audience is not visible — it can only be inferred from the quality of the silence on the other end of the signal. Manona, who had learned to listen as a cattle herder in Durban Location, who had been trained in the discipline of attention by the mission school and Healdtown, now practised listening as a profession. The studio was a different kind of commonage. The microphone was a different kind of cattle camp. But the skill was the same: the labour of noticing what others missed.

He was also a producer. This meant that he did not merely read what others had written. He shaped the content. He selected the music. He structured the programme. He made decisions about what his audience would hear and how they would hear it. In a state institution, these decisions were constrained — the SABC was not a free press — but within the constraints, there was craft. And Manona, who would later become a meticulous researcher, brought craft to everything he touched.

The SABC years also gave him something that the classroom could not: mobility. A teacher was bound to a school. A broadcaster could move — between studios, between towns, between audiences. This mobility would later become essential to his work as an anthropologist, when the roads of the Eastern Cape became his laboratory.

IV. The Translator

While Manona was working at the SABC, he was also doing something else — something that would, decades later, produce one of the most significant posthumous honours of his career. He was translating.

The translation work that would eventually bring him the Sol Plaatje Translation Award from the English Academy of Southern Africa in 2024 (Wits University Press, 2024) was the translation of DDT Jabavu's isiXhosa travelogue, E-Indiya nase East Africa, into English. The story of how this translation came to be is itself a story of intellectual networks and quiet generosity.

Davidson Don Tengo Jabavu — professor of African languages and Latin at the University of Fort Hare, Methodist lay preacher, and one of the leading black intellectuals of the early twentieth century — had travelled to India in November 1949 to attend the World Pacifist Meeting. The conference, held in Santiniketan and Sevagram, brought together delegates from over thirty countries to reflect on how Mahatma Gandhi's principles of non-violence could inform pacifist work in the post-World War II era. Jabavu, who had been issued a passport only at the very last moment by reluctant apartheid officials, wrote an isiXhosa account of his four-month journey — up the east coast of Africa, across the Indian Ocean, through India, and back via Kenya and Uganda. The travelogue was published in 1951 by Lovedale Press (Jabavu, 1951; Wits University Press, 2020).

The text was, as Professor Catherine Higgs would later explain, "a key moment of transition in modern world history" (Wits University Press, 2024). It captured Jabavu's reflections on Gandhian principles, on the dangers of nationalism coupled with militarism and racism, and on the lessons that non-violent resistance might yield for oppressed South Africans. It was, in every sense, a political text — an African intellectual writing from the intersection of the anti-colonial struggle, the civil rights movement, and the Gandhian tradition.

But the text was written in an old isiXhosa orthography, and it was not easily accessible to contemporary readers. Professor Higgs, who had published a biography of Jabavu titled The Ghost of Equality in the 1990s, had commissioned Manona to translate the travelogue into English. The translation was completed, but it sat — for years — in the archive. It was only when Professor Tina Steiner of Stellenbosch University, inspired by Professor Isabel Hofmeyr's work on the Indian Ocean and African intellectual networks, began searching for an English translation that Manona's work was rediscovered (Stellenbosch University, 2020).

The volume was published in 2020 by Wits University Press, with the original isiXhosa text (updated to contemporary orthography by Dr Mhlobo Jadezweni) alongside Manona's English translation. The editors — Steiner, Jadezweni, Higgs, and Evan Mwangi — included a chapter titled "In Praise of Cecil Wele Manona, 1937–2013" (Higgs, 2020). The book was also published by NYU Press in North America, making the travelogue available to an international audience (NYU Press, 2020).

When the Sol Plaatje Translation Award was announced in 2024 — more than a decade after Manona's death — the editors released a statement: "The Sol Plaatje Translation Award is a fitting tribute to Dr Manona's thoughtful and nuanced engagement with this narrative. It is thanks to Dr Manona's intellectual generosity that this little-known but intriguing story of Jabavu's travels in India and Eastern Africa is now accessible to a wider audience" (Wits University Press, 2024). Mrs Nobantu Manona, his wife, had given permission for the translation to be used. The award was posthumous. The recognition was permanent.

The translation work matters for what it tells us about Manona. He was not merely a broadcaster. He was a man of languages — isiXhosa, English, and the space between them. He understood that translation was not a mechanical act of converting words from one tongue to another. It was an act of carrying meaning across a gap — the gap between languages, between cultures, between worlds. The translator, like the broadcaster, like the anthropologist, is a person who stands at the interface and carries what he hears from one side to the other. Manona had been preparing for this work his entire life.

V. The Turn

While Manona was at the SABC, he was doing something else as well — something that would, eventually, redirect the entire course of his professional life. In his spare time, he completed a Bachelor of Arts with honours in Anthropology through the University of South Africa (UNISA) (Whisson, 2013; Wits University Press, 2024). He earned his BA in anthropology from UNISA in 1973 (Wits University Press, 2024).

The decision to study anthropology — and to do so by correspondence, in his spare time, while working full-time at the SABC — reveals something essential about Manona's character. He was not a man who waited for institutions to open doors. He opened them himself. The SABC was his livelihood. UNISA was his vocation. The two existed side by side, the one funding the other, the one feeding the other. The broadcaster who spent his evenings reading anthropology was the same man who had herded cattle as a boy and then walked into a classroom as a teacher. He was always working. He was always learning. The two were, for him, inseparable.

The anthropology degree brought him to the attention of two men who would change his life. The first was Philip Mayer, the head of the Rhodes University Anthropology Department. Mayer was one of the most influential anthropologists of the Eastern Cape, known for his work on Xhosa urban migration and the distinction between "Red" (traditionalist) and "School" (educated) Xhosa identities. His book Townsmen or Tribesmen, published in 1961, was a foundational text of South African urban anthropology (Mayer, 1961). Mayer recognised in Manona something that the academy valued but could not easily produce: a scholar who moved fluently between the world of the location and the world of the seminar room.

The second was Jeff Opland, the energetic and creative Director of the ISER. Opland was building a research programme that drew on the ISER's legacy — the Keiskammahoek Rural Survey, the Border Regional Survey — and extended it into new territory: oral tradition, African music, development studies, and the social conditions of the Eastern Cape under apartheid. He needed researchers who knew the Eastern Cape from the inside. Manona was exactly that researcher (Whisson, 2013).

In 1975, Manona joined the ISER as a research assistant (Wits University Press, 2024). He would remain at the ISER for more than twenty-five years, earning three advanced degrees and retiring as a senior research officer. The broadcaster became the anthropologist. The studio gave way to the field. The microphone gave way to the notebook. But the skill — the skill of listening, of attending, of carrying what he heard with accuracy and care — remained the same.

VI. The Voice That Was Found

What had Manona found in the years between Healdtown and the ISER? He had found his voice — not in the singular sense of a personal style, but in the deeper sense of a vocation. The voice he found was the voice of a man who understood that his task was not to speak but to listen, not to perform but to record, not to interpret from above but to carry from within.

The teacher had learned that education under Bantu Education was a form of control. The broadcaster had learned that the radio was a space where black voices could reach black audiences, even within the constraints of a state institution. The translator had learned that meaning could be carried across the gap between languages with sufficient care. The student of anthropology had learned that the discipline of attention — the discipline he had first learned in the cattle camps of Durban Location — could become a profession.

Each of these lessons was a form of the same lesson. Each was a variation on the theme that had been sounding since his childhood in Peddie: that listening is labour, that words carry weight, and that the scholar's task is not to speak for others but to listen with sufficient care that their voices can carry.

When Manona walked into the ISER in 1975, he brought all of this with him. He brought the mission school's discipline of speech. He brought Healdtown's conviction that education was service. He brought the SABC's training in voice and presence. He brought the translator's skill in carrying meaning across gaps. He brought the anthropologist's commitment to evidence. And he brought the cattle herder's foundational skill — the skill that none of these institutions could teach, because he had learned it before he entered any of them: the labour of noticing what others missed.

He was ready for the field. The Eastern Cape was waiting.

SOURCE / FACT-CHECK NOTE

Key points independently checked for this handover include Manona's 1960s SABC Xhosa-radio career, his 1973 UNISA anthropology degree, his 1975 entry into ISER, and the 2024 posthumous Sol Plaatje Translation Award for his translation of D.D.T. Jabavu's travelogue. See the accompanying research record for final publication citations and source links.`.split("\n\n");

function withArticleOneImages(blocks: string[]): ContentBlock[] {
  return blocks.flatMap((block) => {
    const heading = block.trim().toLowerCase();
    const imageByHeading: Record<string, ContentBlock> = {
      "ii. peddie: frontier, settlement, and the moral geography of manona's birth": {
        type: "image",
        value: PeddieLandscape2,
        alt: "Peddie landscape in the Eastern Cape",
        caption: "The Peddie landscape in the Eastern Cape — the historical world into which Wele Cecil Manona was born in 1937.",
      },
      "the fort and the wars": {
        type: "image",
        value: fortpeddie,
        alt: "Fort Peddie",
        caption: "Fort Peddie, established during the frontier-war period and central to the military history of the district.",
      },
      "the xhosa presence and the resistance": {
        type: "image",
        value: Maqoma,
        alt: "Chief Maqoma",
        caption: "Chief Maqoma, one of the most prominent Xhosa leaders of the nineteenth-century frontier wars.",
      },
      "the milkwood tree: the oath and the education": {
        type: "image",
        value: milkwod5,
        alt: "Historic milkwood tree near Peddie",
        caption: "The historic milkwood tree associated with the 1835 Mfengu/Fingo Oath near Peddie.",
      },
      "the lived rural world: cattle, land, and self-sufficiency": {
        type: "image",
        value: fortpeddie,
        alt: "Rural Peddie landscape with cattle",
        caption: "The rural landscape of Peddie — cattle, commonage and land formed part of the social and economic world of Manona's childhood.",
      },
      "v. the threshold": {
        type: "image",
        value: healdtown,
        alt: "Healdtown Mission Institution",
        caption: "Healdtown Mission Institution, where Manona continued his education after his early schooling in Peddie.",
      },
    };
    const image = imageByHeading[heading];
    return image ? [block, image] : [block];
  });
}

